.TH PATCH 1 2026-10-31 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
patch \- apply a unified diff to files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B patch
.RB [ \-ENR ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR patchfile ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RI [ file
.RI [ patchfile ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B patch
applies the changes in a unified diff, the format that
.BR diff (1)
writes, to the files that the diff names.
It is the
.BR busybox (1)
applet, which is much simpler than GNU
.BR patch ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
The diff is read from standard input, from the file named by
.BR \-i ,
or from the second operand,
.IR patchfile ,
which is used only when there is a first operand.
A
.I patchfile
of
.B \-
is standard input.
Only unified diffs are understood.
Outside a hunk, a line that starts with
.B \-\-\-
or
.B +++
and a space is a file header, and a line that starts with
.B "@@ \-"
after a
.B +++
line is a hunk header.
Every other line outside a hunk is ignored, so text before a diff and
the extra header lines of a git diff do nothing.
A git diff that only renames a file or changes its mode has no effect,
and neither has a binary patch.
.PP
A hunk ends when it has as many lines as its header counts.
An empty line in a hunk is a context line for an empty line.
If a line that does not start with a space,
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
or
.B \e
comes first, or the diff ends first, the hunk fails.
A line that starts with
.B \e
marks the line before it, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and is ignored when it is the first line of a hunk.
.SS "File names"
The file to change is named on the
.B +++
line of its header.
The exception is a file that the diff empties, described below, which is
named on the
.B \-\-\-
line.
A name ends at the first tab, so a time stamp after a tab is not part of
it, and a name may contain spaces.
A time stamp that starts with a year from 1901 to 1970 makes the name
.IR /dev/null ,
which stands for a file that does not exist.
.PP
Leading directories are removed from the name as
.B \-p
says.
If
.I file
is given, it is the file for every section of the diff, and the names in
the diff are not used, not even to recognize
.IR /dev/null .
.SS "Applying hunks"
.B patch
ignores the line numbers in hunk headers, except in a hunk with no
context lines, described below.
It copies the file to a temporary file in the same directory.
For each hunk, in order, it searches forward from the end of the
previous hunk for a place where the hunk's context lines and removed
lines occur together, and writes the hunk's context lines and added
lines there.
Lines must be equal exactly, except that a CR before an LF is ignored.
There are no offsets, no fuzz, and no leniency about white space.
Hunks therefore must be in the same order in the diff as in the file.
A hunk with no trailing context lines, or with fewer trailing than
leading context lines, matches only at the end of the file.
The exception is a hunk with no context lines at either end, such as
every hunk that
.B "diff \-U0"
writes.
It has nothing to search for, so it applies only where the line numbers
in its header put it, counting the lines of the file before it is
patched, and fails if its removed lines are not there.
.PP
When all the hunks of a file have applied, the temporary file replaces
the file.
When a hunk cannot be applied,
.B patch
reports it, leaves the file unchanged, even if earlier hunks applied,
skips the rest of the diff's hunks for that file, and goes on to the
next file.
The exit status is then 1.
.SS "Creating and removing files"
A file is created when the
.B \-\-\-
line names
.IR /dev/null ,
or when the old range of its first hunk is exactly
.BR \-0,0 ,
as in
.BR "@@ \-0,0 +1,3 @@" ,
and the file does not exist.
.B patch
creates any missing directories above it.
When the
.B \-\-\-
line names
.IR /dev/null ,
the file must not already exist, even as an empty file.
Otherwise a first hunk with the old range
.B \-0,0
adds its lines at the top of a file that exists, as
.B "diff \-U0"
means it to.
.PP
The diff empties a file when the
.B +++
line names
.IR /dev/null .
.B patch
truncates the file to zero length, or with
.B \-E
removes it.
It does not compare the file with the hunks, so any file with that name
is emptied or removed, and it ignores the rest of the file's hunks.
A hunk with the new range
.B +0,0
is applied like any other: it removes lines from the top of the file.
.SS Messages
.B patch
prints one line on standard output for each file it processes, with the
name as left by
.BR \-p :
.TP
.BI "patching file " name
The file is patched, or emptied.
.TP
.BI "creating " name
The file is created.
.TP
.BI "removing " name
The file is removed because of
.B \-E
and a
.B +++
line that names
.IR /dev/null .
.PP
The other messages go to standard error:
.TP
.BI "Possibly reversed hunk " n " at " line
Hunk
.I n
of the file, counting from 1, cannot be applied, and a line that it adds
was found in the file where it would add it.
.I line
is the number of that line in the file.
.TP
.BI "Hunk " n " FAILED " old / new .\&
Hunk
.I n
of the file could not be applied.
.I old
and
.I new
are the start lines in its header.
The lines of the hunk follow, as they are in the diff, ending before any
invalid line.
Only the first failed hunk of a file is reported.
.TP
.BI "patch: " message
An error.
.B patch
ends at once.
Files that are already finished stay changed, and a file in progress is
left unchanged, with its temporary file deleted.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-Np0 .
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-p1 ,
or be the next argument.
Long options may be abbreviated to a unique prefix, and the argument of
a long option may follow
.B =
or be the next argument.
Options may follow the operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Options not listed here are errors.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fIN\fR, \fB\-\-strip=\fIN\fR
Remove the first
.I N
components from the file names in the diff.
A component is a name and the slashes after it, and a leading slash is a
component by itself, so
.B \-p1
turns
.I /a/b/c
into
.IR a/b/c ,
and
.I a/b/c
into
.IR b/c .
The last component is never removed, so a name with
.I N
or fewer components keeps its last one.
Without
.BR \-p ,
every directory is removed, leaving the last component: the file is
looked for in the current directory.
.B \-p0
keeps the names as they are.
.I N
is a decimal number, and must not be negative.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR \fIpatchfile\fR, \fB\-\-input=\fIpatchfile\fR
Read the diff from
.I patchfile
instead of standard input.
A
.I patchfile
of
.B \-
is standard input.
This takes the place of a
.I patchfile
operand, which is then ignored.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-reverse
Reverse the diff: remove the lines it adds, and restore the lines it
removes.
The file is still the one on the
.B +++
line.
See
.B NOTES
for diffs that create or remove files.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-forward
Skip a hunk that is already applied, rather than fail.
When the file matches a hunk up to its first added line (a
.B +
line, or with
.B \-R
a
.B \-
line), and the next line of the file equals that line,
.B patch
takes the hunk as applied: it skips the hunk if the rest of the file
matches the patched text, and otherwise applies the hunk as usual.
.BR diff (1)
writes the removed lines of a change first, so a hunk whose first change
replaces lines is recognized only with
.BR \-R .
A hunk with no context lines at either end is never recognized.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-remove\-empty\-files
Remove a file that the diff empties, instead of leaving it empty.
A file whose
.B +++
line names
.I /dev/null
is removed with the message
.BR removing ,
and any other file that is empty once patched is removed after its
.B patching file
message.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Change nothing: create, truncate, remove, and replace no files, and
leave no temporary files.
The messages are as usual, a failed hunk still sets exit status 1, and
a file to be patched must exist.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unified
Accepted and ignored.
Only unified diffs are understood anyway.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Accepted and ignored.
.B patch
never asks a question.
.TP
\fB\-g\fR \fIn\fR, \fB\-\-get=\fIn\fR
Accepted and ignored, with its argument.
.TP
.B \-\-backup\-if\-mismatch
Accepted and ignored.
.B patch
makes no backup files.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-backup\-if\-mismatch
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when
.B patch
starts, as needed when it is run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
The file mode creation mask, in octal.
When it includes the owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
the files that
.B patch
creates are read-only.
A file that it patches is read-only if the old file was.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every hunk applied or was skipped by
.BR \-N ,
or the diff had none.
.TP
.B 1
A hunk failed, a file could not be replaced, or an error ended the
program: an invalid command line,
with a usage message on standard error, an argument of
.B \-p
that is not a number or is negative, a hunk header whose old and new
ranges are both empty, a file that cannot be opened, or one that cannot
be created because it exists.
Also an error writing standard output other than a closed pipe, which is
reported on standard error; the files are patched all the same.
.SH EXAMPLES
Apply a diff made from a source tree, from the top of the tree:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ patch \-p1 <fix.patch
patching file src/main.c
patching file src/util.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check that a diff applies, then apply it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ patch \-\-dry\-run \-p1 \-i fix.patch && patch \-p1 \-i fix.patch
.EE
.RE
.PP
Undo a diff:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ patch \-R \-p1 \-i fix.patch
.EE
.RE
.PP
Apply a diff to a file with a different name than the diff gives:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ diff \-u hello.c.orig hello.c >hello.diff
$ patch hello.c.new hello.diff
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR patch :
.IP \(bu 2
Only unified diffs are understood.
.IP \(bu 2
Hunks are found by content alone, with no fuzz and no offsets.
.IP \(bu 2
A failed hunk gives up on its whole file, where GNU
.B patch
applies the other hunks.
.IP \(bu 2
Neither backup nor reject files are made, and a failed hunk is only
printed.
.IP \(bu 2
It asks no questions, and warns about a reversed hunk only when the
hunk fails.
.IP \(bu 2
The exit status is never 2.
.IP \(bu 2
Options such as
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-F ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-r ,
and
.B \-s
are errors.
.PP
With
.BR \-R ,
the two header lines trade places for creating and removing files, and
so do the two ranges of a hunk: a diff whose
.B \-\-\-
line names
.I /dev/null
empties the file named on the
.B +++
line, or removes it with
.BR \-E ,
and a diff whose
.B +++
line names
.I /dev/null
creates the file named on the
.B \-\-\-
line, as described above.
.PP
Files are read and written in binary mode.
A CR before an LF is ignored in the diff and in the file when lines are
compared, so a diff with CR LF line endings applies to a file with LF
endings, and the reverse.
The lines that
.B patch
copies from the file keep their line endings, and the context and added
lines that it writes from the diff end with CR LF if the first line of
the file does, and with LF otherwise.
So a file whose lines all end the same way keeps its line endings,
whatever those of the diff.
.PP
A line in a hunk that is followed by the marker
.B \e No newline at end of file
is written without a newline.
A last line that lacks a newline gets one when
.B patch
writes it and its line in the hunk has no such marker.
.PP
A name in the diff may be a Windows path.
Only a forward slash separates the components that
.B \-p
removes, so a backslash is part of a component, and a drive letter such
as
.B C:
is a component of its own.
Missing directories are created only above a name that has a forward
slash.
.I /dev/null
is recognized by that spelling only, and not as
.IR nul .
File names, including those in the diff, are UTF-8 and may contain any
Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and later, and are limited
to the system code page on earlier versions.
.PP
The temporary file is named after the file with six more characters.
It replaces the file by a rename, so the result is a new file: it is
read-only only if the old one was, and other hard links to the old file
keep the old content.
If the rename fails, as when another program has the file open,
.B patch
reports it and deletes the temporary file, the file stays unchanged, and
the exit status is 1.
.PP
The file is read one octet at a time up to the end of the last hunk,
which is slow for a large file: a hunk near the end of a file of 7 MB
takes several seconds, and a failed hunk with context lines reads the
rest of the file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR diff (1),
.BR unix2dos (1)
